body:
When multiple indexes with the same field name have different types on those fields, and ES|QL is used to query multiple indexes, ES|QL will return those fields as type `unsupported` with all values set to `null`. Kibana likes to keep clean tables, so it sends `drop_null_columns` along with the query. This removes the entire column of nulls. Users get confused. To help reduce confusion, ES|QL returns an extra header called `all_columns` which includes the missing column, and also lists the `original_types` for the column switched to `unsupported` so that Kibana can explain to the users why that column has `null` values.

If `drop_null_columns` is not sent, the original columns will have the unsupported type, but will also include the `original_types` field:

```
    {
      "name": "metric.value",
      "type": "unsupported",
      "original_types": [
        "double",
        "keyword"
      ],
      "suggested_cast": "keyword"
    }
```

Whether `drop_null_columns` is used or not, this `original_types` information is useful for users to make sense of what is going on. In Kibana, there are warning icons with additional information provided:

<img width="397" height="326" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b3a9e80a-4a73-4cb6-916a-1ab93e6633de" />

However, when the indexes in question have `index.mode:time_series` and one of the field mappings includes `time_series_metric`, then the `original_types` is not provided, and Kibana cannot help the users, leading to much confusion on the part of the user. In Kibana there are no warnings on the fields:

<img width="303" height="80" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7d77c2d2-73b0-46ec-9b5a-a8bff72ded0d" />

### Reproduction

```
PUT /metrics-counter
{
 "settings": {
   "index.mode": "time_series",
   "index.routing_path": [ "metric.name" ]
 },
 "mappings": {
   "properties": {
     "@timestamp": { "type": "date" },
     "metric.name": {
       "type": "keyword",
       "time_series_dimension": true
     },
     "metric.value": {
       "type": "double",
       "time_series_metric": "counter"
     }
   }
 }
}


PUT /metrics-gauge
{
 "settings": {
   "index.mode": "time_series",
   "index.routing_path": [ "metric.name" ]
 },
 "mappings": {
   "properties": {
     "@timestamp": { "type": "date" },
     "metric.name": {
       "type": "keyword",
       "time_series_dimension": true
     },
     "metric.value": {
       "type": "double",
       "time_series_metric": "gauge"
     }
   }
 }
}


PUT /metrics-keyword
{
 "settings": {
   "index.mode": "time_series",
   "index.routing_path": [ "metric.name" ]
 },
 "mappings": {
   "properties": {
     "@timestamp": { "type": "date" },
     "metric.name": {
       "type": "keyword",
       "time_series_dimension": true
     },
     "metric.value": {
       "type": "keyword"
     }
   }
 }
}


POST /metrics-counter/_doc
{
 "@timestamp": "2026-01-20T11:00:00Z",
 "metric.name": "network_packets_total",
 "metric.value": 50
}


POST /metrics-counter/_doc
{
 "@timestamp": "2026-01-20T11:05:00Z",
 "metric.name": "network_packets_total",
 "metric.value": 100
}


POST /metrics-gauge/_doc
{
 "@timestamp": "2026-01-20T10:00:00Z",
 "metric.name": "cpu_usage_percentage",
 "metric.value": 50
}


POST /metrics-gauge/_doc
{
 "@timestamp": "2026-01-20T10:05:00Z",
 "metric.name": "cpu_usage_percentage",
 "metric.value": 100
}


POST /metrics-keyword/_doc
{
 "@timestamp": "2026-01-20T10:00:00Z",
 "metric.name": "cpu_usage_percentage",
 "metric.value": "a"
}


POST /metrics-keyword/_doc
{
 "@timestamp": "2026-01-20T10:05:00Z",
 "metric.name": "cpu_usage_percentage",
 "metric.value": "b"
}

// With drop_null_columns, the metric.value column is removed, and all_columns is missing original_types

POST /_query?drop_null_columns
{
    "query": """
        FROM metrics-gauge, metrics-keyword"""
}

// Without drop_null_columns, the metric-value column is all nulls, and original_types is missing

POST /_query
{
    "query": """
        FROM metrics-gauge, metrics-keyword"""
}

// The above queries mixed a time_series_metric with a normal keyword, but this problem also occurs between two time_series_metric fields

POST /_query?drop_null_columns
{
    "query": """
        FROM metrics-gauge, metrics-counter"""
}

// Also happens with wildcards

POST /_query?drop_null_columns
{
    "query": "FROM metrics-*"
}
```

If we change the index settings to not have `index-mode:time_series` or we remove the `time_series_metric` from the fields, then the missing `original_types` come back.
